From: zhugengyu <zhugengyu2023@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Port the AIC8800 demo stack from raw FreeRTOS APIs to the
 CherryUSB OSAL

The reference implementation (qwer-zxcv/CherryUSB pr/aic8800-wifi-demo,
commit 084fb84) was validated on STM32H7 + FreeRTOS and reaches for
FreeRTOS primitives directly.  This repository runs the imported stack on
the first-party CMSIS-RTOS2 OSAL over both kernel backends, so the port
maps every primitive onto the OSAL face:

- mutex with timeout -> one-token usb_osal_sem (the OSAL mutex take has
  no timeout argument; the TX path measures its wait);
- queue-of-slot-indices -> usb_osal_mq (uintptr_t tokens), with a
  shadow free/pending depth counter where the reference asked
  uxQueueMessagesWaiting() - the OSAL queue has no count query;
- task-notification wakeup of the RX loop -> counting usb_osal_sem
  (given from completion context; the OSAL give is safe from interrupt
  and thread context alike, so the reference's FromISR split collapses);
- taskENTER/EXIT_CRITICAL -> usb_osal_enter/leave_critical_section with
  a per-function saved state (nesting-safe: every nesting level has its
  own stack slot);
- thread creation/deletion -> usb_osal_thread_create/delete, stack
  sizes converted from FreeRTOS words to bytes (768w -> 3072B, 512w ->
  2048B), priorities onto the OSAL band (smaller = more urgent; the
  stack's own threads sit at 0: boot 3, FMAC worker 4, data TX 6, ping
  10);
- DWT->CYCCNT / xTaskGetTickCount timing -> rtos_clock_us() (the AArch64
  system counter); every statistic the reference kept in cycles is now
  microseconds, and the ACK-pace threshold converts from
  SystemCoreClock/Hz to 1000000/Hz microseconds;
- __WEAK (cmsis_compiler.h in the reference's STM32 world) -> the GCC
  weak attribute, stated in aic8800_fw.c and aic8800_lwip.c directly
  (this port's include paths carry no CMSIS compiler header);
- the STM32H7 board specifics in log lines (HyperRAM address) and the
  DWT/CoreDebug enable block are dropped.

Everything else - the FMAC protocol, the WPA2 path, the TX queue
discipline (ACK hold + priority reserve + global ordering) - is the
reference's bytes, untouched.
